; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Sparse Starfield
; CATEGORY: Lists & Data Structures
; LEVEL:    Advanced
; TAGS:     sparse array, property lists, memory, grids, dot
; SUMMARY:  Stores a huge 1000 by 1000 sky by remembering only the cells that hold a star, then draws it and answers questions about it.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   A full 1000 by 1000 grid would need a million boxes, but the sky
;   has only a few hundred stars. A sparse array remembers just the
;   cells that are not empty. Here each star is stored on a property
;   list whose property name is made from its row and column, like
;   r12c40. Asking for any other cell gives 0, as if it were there.
;   The program scatters stars, draws them, and counts stars inside
;   a box.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run. Change HOWMANY to put more stars in the sky. The
;   numbers printed show how much memory the sparse version saves.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * a sparse array: store only what is not empty
;   * building keys with WORD so one plist can act like a table
;   * a default value for missing entries
;   * PLIST to list everything that is stored
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Make SKYSET with brightness 0 remove the star with REMPROP.
;   * Draw a constellation by joining the five brightest stars.
; ============================================================

make "howmany 300

; The key for a cell is a word such as r12c40.
to cellkey :row :col
  output (word "r :row "c :col)
end

; Each stored value is a list [row col brightness]. Keeping the row
; and column inside the value means we never have to pick the key
; word apart again when we want to draw the star.
to skyset :row :col :brightness
  pprop "sky cellkey :row :col (list :row :col :brightness)
end

; Missing cells come back from GPROP as the empty list. We turn that
; into 0 so the sky behaves like a full grid filled with zeros.
to skyget :row :col
  localmake "v gprop "sky cellkey :row :col
  if emptyp :v [output 0]
  output item 3 :v
end

; PLIST "sky is [key value key value ...], so we keep just the values.
to stars
  localmake "pl plist "sky
  localmake "out []
  while [not emptyp :pl] [
    make "out fput first butfirst :pl :out
    make "pl butfirst butfirst :pl
  ]
  output :out
end

to drawsky
  foreach stars [
    star (item 2 ?) * 0.8 - 400 300 - (item 1 ?) * 0.6 item 3 ?
  ]
end

to star :x :y :b
  setpencolor item :b [[90 90 140] [150 150 200] [210 210 240] [255 255 180] [255 255 255]]
  setpensize :b + 1
  dot list :x :y
end

; Count the stars inside a rectangle of rows and columns. INBOX? can
; see :r1 and the others because Logo uses dynamic scope: a procedure
; sees the inputs of the procedure that called it. We test the
; few hundred stored stars instead of the ten thousand cells in the
; box, which is the whole point of a sparse array.
to countbox :r1 :r2 :c1 :c2
  output count filter [inbox? ?] stars
end

to inbox? :s
  output (and (item 1 :s) >= :r1 (item 1 :s) <= :r2 (item 2 :s) >= :c1 (item 2 :s) <= :c2)
end

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground [10 10 40]
(rerandom 2026)
repeat :howmany [skyset 1 + random 999 1 + random 999 1 + random 5]
print sentence [A full grid would store this many cells:] 1000 * 1000
print sentence [The sparse sky stores only:] count stars
print sentence [Brightness at row 500, column 500:] skyget 500 500
print sentence [Stars in rows 1-100, columns 1-100:] countbox 1 100 1 100
drawsky
